| Load this file and press ^N to see what happens!

| This is version 1 of the teleprompter code. There is also a version 2,
| lib/teleprompter2.mu4, which compiles stories into colon words instead of
| leaving them as text. I think this version is simpler, more flexible, and
| less surprising to the reader.

| This code makes it easy to paste into the command line, one by one and in
| sequence, previously-written chunks of text. It can be especially useful
| for making screencasts, so that the viewer isn't stuck watching the
| author painfully type and correct. Line-by-line tutorials, including code
| to try out or edit and change, are easy too.
|
| A group of lines - or prompts - is called a story, and each story, like
| any other word in muforth, has a name and can be executed; executing a
| story pushes it onto the story stack and makes it the current story.
| Every story starts and ends with an implicit empty line. The starting
| empty line has itself as its previous line; the ending line has itself as
| its next line. You have to type ^N after executing a story to see its
| first line.
|
| The teleprompter interacts with the command line history in the following
| way. Each time ^N (or ^P) is pressed, the next (or previous) teleprompter
| line is pasted into the command line buffer, but not executed. It's
| exactly like what happens using the up and down arrow keys to explore the
| command-line history.
|
| If a pasted teleprompter text line looks like code, pressing return will
| execute it and enter it into the history buffer. The user has complete
| control. This is an advantage for screencasting use, but a minor
| disadvantage for tutorial use, since the novice user can't tell what is
| explanatory text and what is code to be executed. To make this
| distinction clearer, one could begin the non-code lines of a tutorial
| with the "line comment" character, "|", or annotate code lines with a
| trailing comment telling the user to press return to execute the code.
|
| Since command lines can only be 255 characters long it makes sense to use
| a byte-sized - rather than cell-sized - count, just as with command-line
| history.
|
| In its "compiled" form, each line of text starts on a cell boundary and
| consists of the following parts:
|
|   - byte-sized *cell* offset to the previous line
|   - byte-sized *cell* offset to the next line
|   - byte-sized "type" - currently ignored
|   - byte-sized (< 256) text length
|   - bytes of the text, padded at the end to a cell boundary
|
| There is no zero terminator.
|
| I chose cell offsets for next and previous to increase the range. Since
| next and prev are treated as signed offsets, prompts would need to be
| less than 128 bytes long to be addressable (actually even less, because
| of the padding, pointers, and count byte). A byte-sized, signed cell
| offset is compact but gives us the range that we need.

| We want to have a "stack" of stories, so they can refer to each other,
| and when a new story is "executed", it pushes a stack instead of simply
| replacing the current story.
|
| The story stack is a full ascending stack. This means that 'story points
| to the last-pushed story. We have to be careful to make sure that the
| stack always contains a valid "root" story.

16 array story-stack
variable 'story      | pointer to current story in the stack

: empty-story-stack   story-stack cell- 'story ! ;

: push-story  ( s)     'story @                          cell+ tuck !  'story ! ;
: pop-story   ( - s)   'story @  story-stack over u< if  cell- dup     'story !  then  @ ;

| Teleprompter pointer.
|
| tp always points to the first byte of a compiled prompt: the offset to
| the previous line.

: tp  ( - line)  'story @ ;      | teleprompter pointer; points into the current story

| This version returns the type byte. For future use.
| tp>text  ( 'prompt - type a u)   2 + ( skip prev+next)  c@+ c@+ swap  ( type a len) ;

| This version ignores the type byte. This is what we are currently using.
: tp>text  ( 'prompt - a u)   3 + ( skip prev+next+type)  c@+ swap  ( a len) ;

: tp-show-prompt  ( len idx key 'prompt - len' idx' exit?)
   nip nip nip  tp>text  tuck cl-show  dup 0 ;

| Pop the story stack and continue where we left off.
: tp-pop   ( len idx key - len' idx' exit?)  pop-story  tp-show-prompt ;

| Show the next or previous prompt. dir specifies the direction.
: tp-show  ( len idx key dir - len' idx' exit?)
   tp @  tuck + c@  "80 sign-extend cells +  ( prev or next tp)  dup tp !
   tp-show-prompt ;

| Hook into the editline code. When we see a ^N, move to the next prompt
| line and show it; when we see ^P, show the previous prompt line.

-: ( len idx key - len' idx' exit?)
   dup ctrl N = if  1 tp-show  ^  then
   dup ctrl P = if  0 tp-show  ^  then
   dup ctrl R = if    tp-pop   ^  then  drop 0 ;  is cl-key-unmatched

| Create a new sequence of prompts - a story.
: story
   create  ( - prev)  here  1  over 1+  0 ,  c!  ( 0 prev offset, +1 next offset)
   does> push-story ;

| End a story.
: fin  ( prev)  here tuck - cell/ swap  0 ,  c!  ( prev offset, 0 next offset) ;

| Compile a new text line, with forward and backward links (byte-sized cell
| offsets) and a type byte.

: text,  ( prev type - prev')
   here push  ( pointer to the start of this entry)
   swap  r@ - push  ( compute prev offset)  push  ( type)
   #LF parse  ( a u)  255 min  ( limit length to fit in a byte)
   dup  dup 4 + aligned  dup allot  ( allot space for 3 counts+type+string, cell-aligned)
   0 here cell- !  ( zero out last cell to remove any junk; do this before compiling
                     the pointers and the text so small texts don't get clobbered!)
   cell/  pop swap  pop cell/  ( a len len type next prev)
   r@ c!+  ( prev offset)  c!+  ( next offset)  c!+ ( type)  c!+ ( length byte)
   swap cmove  ( copy string into dict)  pop  ( new prev) ;

| We distinguish between different types of prompt lines. The "type" byte
| gets compiled into the line, but currently doesn't change the behavior
| (ie, execute or not) or styling (eg, color) of the line. Having two (or
| more) line types allows authors to express their intentions, and someday
| this code might do something with the embedded type info!

: ~|  ( prev - prev')  0 text, ;    | explanatory text; "|" suggests a comment
: ~>  ( prev - prev')  1 text, ;    | potentially executable text

| Two example stories follow. We set gospel as the "root" story; it gives
| the user the option to read the example-nested-story, to demonstrate how
| story nesting and ^R work.

story example-nested-story
~| You are now reading the example "nested" story.
~| You probably got here from the gospel story.
~| As with any other story, you can press ^N to move forward to the next line,
~| and ^P to move backwards to the previous.
~| If you are reading a *nested* story - like this one - you can press ^R
~| to return to the previous story.
~| Nested stories can be used to create tables of contents, or self-guided,
~| hierarchical tutorials, or anything else you can think of!
~| Press ^R to return to the gospel story.
fin

story gospel
~| In the beginning was the Forth
~| And the Forth was with Chuck
~| And the Forth *was* Chuck
~|    (the very short gospel of Forth)

~| You are reading a teleprompter story: a series of text lines, or prompts.
~| ^N takes you to the next line; ^P to the previous one.
~| When you reach the beginning or the end of a story, a blank line is shown.
~| In a teleprompter story it's possible to execute code as well as show text.
~| Just press return when the command line contains code.
~| If you make a mistake and try to execute something that *isn't* code,
~| you will get a harmless "xyz not found" error. Just press ^N to keep going.

~>    ld lib/rational-approx.mu4  phi   | press return to load and run

~| That line of code loaded a rational approximation library and used it to
~| calculate two integer factors that when divided yield phi (the golden ratio)
~| to 9 decimal digits of precision!
~| By adding a lot of zeros at the end of the numerator we can test the
~| result ourselves, using integer math.
~| The first comma of the result represents the decimal point.

~>    75,025,000,000,000 46,368 / .

~| Now that the rational approximation code is loaded, you can also try

~>    pi

~| As with phi, we can test the result ourselves, to check it:

~>    104,348,000,000,000 33,215 / .

~| And we can do the same with e and the square root of 2:

~>    e
~>    124,288,000,000,000 45,723 / .

~>    sq2
~>    47,321,000,000,000 33,461 / .

~| Teleprompter stories can also be *nested*.

~>    example-nested-story     | press return and then ^N to read it

~| ^R will always take you back to the previous story, if there is one.
~| That's it! Have fun writing - and reading - teleprompter stories!
fin

| To set things up, we first clear the story stack and then execute gospel,
| which pushes itself as the root story. After this, pop-story refuses to
| pop the stack back to the "empty" state, which would cause a crash if the
| user then typed ^N or ^P.
|
| If you want to replace the example root story with one of your own,
| simply write a new story, empty the story stack, and execute *your*
| story.
|
| A root story can be used as a kind of table of contents, linking to other
| stories simply by mentioning them. (Of course, the reader has to
| "execute" the line containing the story reference, in order to make *it*
| the current story.) Likewise, those stories can link to others, making a
| hierarchy of stories. This is why ^R is so important: so the reader can
| return to where they were in the "calling" story.

empty-story-stack  gospel
